Monitoring vs Observability

🔹 What is Monitoring? 📊
 Monitoring is the process of collecting and tracking system metrics like CPU usage, memory, disk, and uptime.
 It helps you answer: 👉 “Is my system healthy or not?”

🔹 What is Observability? 🔍
 Observability goes beyond monitoring. It helps you understand what’s happening inside the system by analyzing:
 ✔ Metrics
 ✔ Logs
 ✔ Traces
It answers: 👉 “Why is my system behaving like this?”

🔹 Key Differences: ⚡

✔ Monitoring
Focus: Known issues
Data: Metrics
Output: Alerts & dashboards

✔ Observability
Focus: Unknown issues
Data: Metrics + Logs + Traces
Output: Root cause analysis

👉 In short:
 Monitoring tells you “Something is wrong”
 Observability tells you “Why it is wrong”

🔹 Why Both Are Needed in Real-World? 🏗️
In production systems, failures are unpredictable.
Monitoring helps you detect issues quickly 🚨
Observability helps you fix issues faster ⚙️
Without monitoring → You don’t know there’s a problem
 Without observability → You can’t solve the problem efficiently
👉 You need BOTH to build reliable systems.

🔹 Real-World Example (CPU Spike Case) 💡
Your application suddenly becomes slow 🐢
👉 Monitoring shows:
CPU usage = 95% 🚨
👉 Observability helps you dig deeper:
Logs show → Too many requests hitting API
Traces show → One service is taking too long
Root cause → Infinite loop or unoptimized code
✅ Now you don’t just see the issue—you understand it and fix it.

🔹 Key Concepts to Remember: 🧠
✔ Monitoring = Detection 📊
 ✔ Observability = Investigation 🔍
 ✔ Metrics show trends
 ✔ Logs show events
 ✔ Traces show request flow
